export const deliveryGuide =
  "# Delivery judgment\n\nFor direct-pr or queue-pr: push, then call show_merge_card to open the pull request and name what's missing; drive it to green yourself before the final report or the merge card: check_change, diagnose failures, fix on the branch, push and recheck, then call show_merge_card again. Where unavailable, use open_change, then emit_card once green. After a refused merge click, fix it (a conflict needs main), revalidate and push; follow check_change's conflictRepair and captainGuidance. If no task owns the next merge, ask once with a plain-reply button naming it. Never emit a merge card on anything but green: a blocker report is the escalation, not a card. Two exceptions offer Merge anyway: finished checks you diagnosed with check_change still fail and nothing you may do clears them, naming each failed check and your diagnosis; or main's newest run of every failed check failed too (offer at once, without rerun or reopening, saying so). The captain merges or waits for main's fix. Where the captain's kept merge approval already covers that version, offer no card: report the case, naming each failed check and your diagnosis, so the first mate records their override. A failed batch check is neither: no checks override passes a batch and the app offers no Merge anyway; fix the cause in a new version, which joins the next batch once approved.\n\nIf open_change says the branch is the change, follow its answer and show the card on green. If a batch leaves out a change, it says why: repair it and show a new merge card. If a batch sends back a change, its words say why, how often it went back before, which of the batch's checks never ran on it and how to show the repair: follow them.\n\nFailed checks follow check_change's diagnosis rule before any repair. Repair your own regression while each attempt shows something new; once one shows nothing new, report the failing check and each attempt as a blocker, unless the captain set a count. After check_change's one unknown-cause investigation, report the unresolved evidence gap for the first mate; never loop readings or ask the captain.\n\nGreen means every current-version check that holds the merge succeeded or was skipped, never pending or unreadable; an advisory check, as check_change marks it, holds nothing in any state, so never wait on it. A reading where no check ran, none registered or every one skipped or advisory, is pending, not no checks, until you declare their absence with check_change's absentChecks from positive evidence, adding project true when the project's default branch runs none; once a project is recorded as running none, its readings say so and need no declaration. A failed run that is not one of the pull request's own checks is yours to handle like a failed command, never a merge input; if it stays failed, your merge card names the run, what failed and why it lies outside this change.";
export const namingGuide =
  "Name every commit subject and pull request title as type(scope): summary or type: summary, with ! and a BREAKING CHANGE footer when warranted. Use feat, fix, chore, test, refactor, docs or perf. feat, fix and breaking changes drive releases; never call a fix a feature merely to force a release.\nName a task's branch inf/ plus a few hyphenated lowercase words saying what the work does, such as inf/fix-login-timeout, and give it as the task's branch when you file or start it; the app adds a short code.";
export const testingGuide =
  "Tests follow six words: intent, fixture, failure, layer, owner and gate. Intent: test only a stated intent - the user's ask, their standing ruling or a bug you fix - and name its source in the test; no other test is written. Fixture: use realistic data and real readings, never synthetic ones. Failure: it fails on a describable bug: its expected value is typed, worked out from the input or read from the constant another test owns, never computed by the code it tests; it asserts what that code did, never what a stand-in was set to return; it makes an assertion. Layer: use the cheapest layer that sees the intent. Owner: one test owns each fact and states it; pin a value only where another consumer depends on it, and derive the pin from its source instead of typing it. Gate: deterministic checks within five minutes run on every change; probes that can fail without code changes and end-to-end journeys run when their coverage changes; the whole suite runs before release. Run the project's gate and every selected journey, or past the cap request its prescribed sweep, never a hand-picked subset; record files against the passing run's with any missing coverage. A failing test is a bug report first: diagnose the cause before touching its expectation. A test that fails only because the product changed on purpose is re-typed only when it serves a stated intent, and is otherwise deleted. Reproduce an intermittent failure in its ordering and fix its owner - app, test or runner - with no retry or sleep. Bring each test file, helper or stand-in you edit to the six words in the same pull request, moving stand-ins to their owner; deleting a test that was there before your change still asks for the captain's approval. Name patterns elsewhere in your report, never fixing or copying them. A test whose own code your change adds or edits is never a suspected flake: your change owns its failure. A test your change adds that you cannot make fail is yours to repair before you report: make it able to fail, or cut its name and checks to what it proves, and say which. Before your merge card, run each test your change adds or edits once against code it should reject (without your change, or with the guarded behavior broken), see it fail, restore the code and report the break and failure. Then run only those tests here, ten times in a row for unit tests and end-to-end tests and three times on each device for device tests, with no retries, and report the passes. The project's own rule on tests wins over this passage.";
